# Review ownership. This file is what turns "signed by an approver" into a
# process: the signature proves who approved, CODEOWNERS proves they were asked.
#
# GitHub branch protection on `main` requires CODEOWNERS review, so none of the
# paths below can change without the named team on the pull request.

*                                   @example-bank/accounts-api-team

# --- security-owned: the controls themselves -------------------------------
/policy/                            @example-bank/security-engineering
/iam/                               @example-bank/security-engineering
/runtime/                           @example-bank/security-engineering
/.github/workflows/                 @example-bank/security-engineering
/THREAT-MODEL.md                    @example-bank/security-engineering
/DECISIONS.md                       @example-bank/security-engineering

# --- the exception path: two teams, deliberately ---------------------------
# A waiver needs the service team (who requested it) and security (who approved
# it) on the same PR. The checker also enforces requested_by != approved_by, so
# the two-person rule holds even if branch protection is misconfigured.
/waivers/waivers.yaml               @example-bank/security-engineering @example-bank/accounts-api-team

# --- security leads only ---------------------------------------------------
# Who may approve waivers is a higher bar than what may be waived. Without this
# line the approver list is self-serve and the whole mechanism is decorative.
/waivers/APPROVERS.yaml             @example-bank/security-leads
/waivers/keys/                      @example-bank/security-leads
/.github/CODEOWNERS                 @example-bank/security-leads

# --- infrastructure --------------------------------------------------------
/terraform/                         @example-bank/security-engineering @example-bank/platform
/k8s/                               @example-bank/platform @example-bank/accounts-api-team
